  "ai_overview": {
    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Treatment for high blood pressure (hypertension) includes heart-healthy lifestyle changes and prescription medications, often managed with a target blood pressure of less than 130/80 mmHg.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "heart-healthy lifestyle changes and prescription medications"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "You can review detailed treatment guidelines through the National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute or consult the Mayo Clinic for diagnostic and management overviews.",
        "snippet_links": [
          {
            "text": "National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute",
            "link": "https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/high-blood-pressure/treatment"
          },
          {
            "text": "Mayo Clinic",
            "link": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/high-blood-pressure/diagnosis-treatment/drc-20373417"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Lifestyle Changes"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Eat a healthy diet: Follow the DASH (Dietary Approaches to Stop Hypertension) diet, which focuses on fruits, vegetables, whole grains, and low-fat dairy while limiting sodium (salt)."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Exercise regularly: Get regular physical activity, such as brisk walking or aerobic exercise."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Maintain a healthy weight: Lose excess weight to reduce strain on the heart."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Limit alcohol: Reduce or avoid drinking alcohol."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Quit smoking: Stop smoking and avoid tobacco products."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Manage stress: Use stress-reduction techniques and get enough sleep."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "First-Line Medications"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Doctors often prescribe one or more of these common drug classes to lower blood pressure:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Thiazide diuretics (\"water pills\"): Help the body remove excess sodium and water."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "ACE inhibitors: Relax blood vessels by blocking a chemical that narrows them."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "ARBs (Angiotensin II receptor blockers): Keep blood vessels relaxed by blocking the effects of angiotensin."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Calcium channel blockers: Relax blood vessels and slow the heart rate."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Treatment Approach by Stage"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Elevated blood pressure (120-129/<80): Treated primarily with lifestyle changes, rechecked in 3 to 6 months."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Stage 1 hypertension (130-139/80-89): Treated with lifestyle changes alone if cardiovascular risk is low; medication is added if risk is 10% or higher, or if diabetes or heart disease is present."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Stage 2 hypertension (140/90 or higher): Treated immediately with a combination of lifestyle changes and two different blood pressure medications."
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
